Nessus Vulnerability Scans — Windows Server 2019
Overview
This folder contains vulnerability scan results from Tenable Nessus Essentials conducted against a Windows Server 2019 virtual machine in my cybersecurity home lab environment. The scans were performed using a Basic Network Scan policy with CVSS v3.0 severity scoring.
A total of 5 scans were conducted between March 12 and March 15, 2026 to identify, remediate, and verify the resolution of security vulnerabilities consistent with DoD Risk Management Framework (RMF) continuous monitoring requirements.

Scan 1 — Initial Assessment (March 12, 2026)
File: Scan-1-Initial-March12.png
Findings:

1 Medium — SMB Signing Not Required (CVSS 5.3)
1 Low — ICMP Timestamp Request Remote Date Disclosure (CVSS 2.1)
18 Informational findings

Summary: Initial baseline scan identified one Medium severity vulnerability related to SMB signing not being enforced which could allow an attacker to conduct man in the middle attacks against SMB traffic. Remediation was prioritized.

Scan 5 — Post Remediation Verification (March 15, 2026)
File: Scan-5-PostRemediation-March15.png
Findings:

0 Medium — SMB Signing finding successfully remediated ✅
1 Low — ICMP Timestamp Request Remote Date Disclosure (residual — accepted risk)
18 Informational findings

Summary: Post remediation scan confirmed the Medium severity SMB Signing vulnerability was successfully resolved through Group Policy configuration. The remaining Low severity ICMP Timestamp finding was documented as an accepted risk in the POA&M.

Scan History Overview
File: Scan-History-Overview.png
Summary: The history tab shows 5 completed scans across the assessment period demonstrating continuous monitoring practices consistent with RMF Step 6 — Monitor. Each scan was reviewed and findings were tracked and documented accordingly.

